Meaning and limits
The synchronous codec work under @concurrent/generic executor calls confirms the source-level concern from the earlier review: an async API may perform substantial synchronous CPU work without yielding. The sample does not measure how long each operation monopolises a worker, show a pending Foundation operation, prove all CPU cores were saturated, or identify GCD's work-admission decision. It cannot establish a hard deadline guarantee, a single root cause, or the value of a proposed QoS change. Parent-reported diagnostic12 late queue canaries invalidate the premise of the earlier conditional broad-deadline-removal suggestion; that suggestion remains unaccepted and is not the current repair recommendation.

Concrete narrow fixture issue revealed alongside this run
CodexR3TransportCloseTests.swift:194 calls listener.acceptConnection(timeoutSeconds:1). JPIPTCPSocket.swift:313–331 does not implement a one-second listener polling interval: it passes that value to the accepted JPIPTCPConnection, installing one-second receive/send idle timeouts. The peer writes the warm keep-alive reply, immediately loops to readHTTPRequest, and exits/closes on any error (test210 onward). If caller scheduling postpones the next request beyond that idle interval, the supposedly reused socket legitimately becomes stale and the transport retries on a second connection. This is a concrete source route to the reused acceptedCount assertion failure; there is no captured errno/timing proof that this exact route occurred in13, so the cause remains probable rather than asserted as observed.

Smallest bounded options
1. For the proven fixture design mismatch, make deliberately held keep-alive peer lifetime depend on explicit test cancellation/close and worker join, with a distinct finite test-failure guard. Preserve the intentional stale-retry mode and all real request deadline checks. This fixes the fixture's idle-expiry assumption without changing production transport policy. Root/writer must approve and validate the exact implementation; this reviewer made no edit.
2. For HTTPS timing, retain current required timeout/interruption assertions while locating the actual queue-delay interval. A sample triggered when a delegate-queue canary is overdue would distinguish active CPU, blocking dependency and idle work-admission behavior more directly than this startup window. Avoid another undirected broad diagnostic.
3. If a narrowly scoped fairness experiment is chosen, test-only yields between completed operations in the sampled fixture loops could discriminate scheduling effects while keeping every case/assertion and full concurrency. Such an experiment is not a production repair or acceptance evidence and Task.yield is not a progress guarantee. Moving entire codec pipelines, disabling concurrency, removing owner joins or changing production QoS is not justified here.

Positive stop
Read-only inspection and review artifacts only. No native command, repository change, timeout/cap increase, commit or push. Exact extracts, source and evidence SHA-256 values accompany this report.
